WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.748
"Self-driving policies learned by behavioral
cloning are usually trained open-loop,

00:00:05.828 --> 00:00:08.509
matching expert actions one step at a time,

00:00:08.589 --> 00:00:13.260
yet they are deployed closed-loop where every
action shapes future states.

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:16.213
This mismatch quietly hurts real-world safety.

00:00:16.293 --> 00:00:18.267
This paper introduces C-W-E-R-M,

00:00:18.347 --> 00:00:23.788
Closed-loop Weighted Empirical Risk Minimization: a
simple two-stage recipe that first

00:00:23.868 --> 00:00:27.832
runs a policy in a simulator to find the scenes
where it fails,

00:00:27.912 --> 00:00:32.134
then upsamples exactly those scenes when training
the final policy.

00:00:32.214 --> 00:00:36.756
On a challenging urban driving dataset it cuts
collisions substantially,

00:00:36.836 --> 00:00:40.672
with improvements around thirty-five percent on
some metrics,

00:00:40.752 --> 00:00:45.230
all without a differentiable simulator or costly
closed-loop training."

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:50.918
"Imitation learning for self-driving cars is
usually done through behavioral cloning,

00:00:50.998 --> 00:00:55.101
where the network is trained to reproduce an
expert's next action.

00:00:55.181 --> 00:01:01.377
The catch is that this is done open-loop: the model
never sees the consequences of its own actions.

00:01:01.457 --> 00:01:03.659
But when the policy actually drives,

00:01:03.739 --> 00:01:06.828
every action changes the future state it will see.

00:01:06.908 --> 00:01:09.046
Small prediction errors accumulate,

00:01:09.126 --> 00:01:14.308
pushing the car into out-of-distribution situations
the model was never trained on.

00:01:14.388 --> 00:01:19.062
And the metrics that truly matter, like collisions,
are non-differentiable,

00:01:19.142 --> 00:01:23.118
so they are effectively invisible to the standard
training loss.

00:01:23.198 --> 00:01:29.140
The result is a policy that looks great open-loop
but drives poorly in closed-loop evaluation."

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:32.366
"There have been attempts to close this gap.

00:01:32.446 --> 00:01:38.018
Methods like Urban Driver run a differentiable
simulator directly inside the training

00:01:38.098 --> 00:01:40.678
loop using backpropagation through time.

00:01:40.758 --> 00:01:45.465
This works, but it is expensive: it requires a
differentiable simulator,

00:01:45.545 --> 00:01:46.995
it does not scale well,

00:01:47.075 --> 00:01:51.982
and it carries the heavy memory cost of unrolling
policies during training.

00:01:52.062 --> 00:01:56.437
Other approaches collect on-policy data or add
extra human oracles,

00:01:56.517 --> 00:01:58.166
which are slow and costly.

00:01:58.246 --> 00:02:04.350
The authors ask a simpler question: can we get
closed-loop benefits by only using a simulator

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:06.943
to decide which training scenes matter,

00:02:07.023 --> 00:02:11.332
without changing the loss function or requiring
differentiability?

00:02:11.412 --> 00:02:13.460
That question motivates CW-ERM."

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:17.513
"The paper makes three contributions.

00:02:17.593 --> 00:02:21.265
First, it proposes Closed-loop Weighted Empirical
Risk Minimization,

00:02:21.345 --> 00:02:25.018
a technique that leverages closed-loop metrics from
policy rollouts to debias the policy

00:02:25.098 --> 00:02:28.771
network and shrink the distribution gap between
open-loop training and closed-loop inference.

00:02:28.851 --> 00:02:32.524
Second,

00:02:32.604 --> 00:02:36.276
it evaluates the method experimentally on a
challenging urban driving dataset and shows

00:02:36.356 --> 00:02:40.029
significant closed-loop improvements,

00:02:40.109 --> 00:02:43.782
all without complex or computationally expensive
closed-loop training.

00:02:43.862 --> 00:02:47.535
Third,

00:02:47.615 --> 00:02:51.287
it establishes an important theoretical connection
between this reweighting scheme and

00:02:51.367 --> 00:02:55.040
the classic family of methods that correct
covariate shift through density-ratio estimation."

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:59.366
"The method has three stages and is strikingly simple.

00:02:59.446 --> 00:03:03.393
In stage one, you train an identification policy
the ordinary way,

00:03:03.473 --> 00:03:07.419
with standard ERM behavioral cloning.

00:03:07.499 --> 00:03:11.445
In stage two,

00:03:11.525 --> 00:03:15.471
you take that policy and roll it out in a
closed-loop simulator over the training scenes,

00:03:15.551 --> 00:03:19.498
collecting metrics like the number of collisions.

00:03:19.578 --> 00:03:23.524
Every scene where a cost is positive, meaning the
policy failed,

00:03:23.604 --> 00:03:27.550
goes into an error set.

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:31.576
In stage three, you train the final policy again
with weighted ERM,

00:03:31.656 --> 00:03:35.603
but now the error-set scenes are upsampled by a factor w.

00:03:35.683 --> 00:03:39.629
This is nearly identical to the original
behavioral-cloning objective,

00:03:39.709 --> 00:03:43.655
with the single addition of a weighting term driven
by closed-loop failures.

00:03:43.735 --> 00:03:47.681
Two practical tricks matter: the identification
policy is stopped early so the error set is not depleted,

00:03:47.761 --> 00:03:51.708
and upsampling is used instead of reweighting
because it is more stable.

00:03:51.788 --> 00:03:55.734
Crucially, because the simulator is only used to
pick scenes,

00:03:55.814 --> 00:03:59.760
any non-differentiable metric works and no
differentiable simulator is needed."

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:04.750
"The method is evaluated on a proprietary
real-world dataset,

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:09.440
collected from the company's self-driving vehicles
on challenging urban missions in San Francisco and Palo Alto.

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:14.130
It includes recorded trajectories of the ego
vehicle and surrounding agents together with high-definition maps.

00:04:14.210 --> 00:04:18.820
The scenarios are diverse and difficult: stopping
behind a lead vehicle,

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:23.510
stopping at intersections, and navigating dense
traffic with cars, pedestrians,

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:28.200
and cyclists.

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:32.890
Most scenes run eleven to thirteen seconds, with
the longest reaching thirty seconds.

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:37.580
In total,

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:42.270
the authors train on one hundred and eighty hours
of driving and validate and test on sixty hours each.

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:46.960
They also open-source the closed-loop simulator and
metrics used in the work."

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:48.858
"The results are strong.

00:04:48.938 --> 00:04:54.252
Compared against the best baseline, behavioral
cloning with ERM and perturbation,

00:04:54.332 --> 00:04:58.047
CW-ERM significantly reduces collisions across the board,

00:04:58.127 --> 00:05:02.642
with improvements reaching about thirty-five
percent on some metrics.

00:05:02.722 --> 00:05:05.838
When the model upsamples front-collision scenes,

00:05:05.918 --> 00:05:08.768
front collisions fall from fourteen to nine.

00:05:08.848 --> 00:05:11.432
When it upsamples side-collision scenes,

00:05:11.512 --> 00:05:14.894
side collisions fall from fifty-five to forty-seven.

00:05:14.974 --> 00:05:20.355
There is a nice side effect too: improving one
metric often improves related ones.

00:05:20.435 --> 00:05:24.084
Upsampling side collisions also reduces rear collisions,

00:05:24.164 --> 00:05:30.077
evidence that the policy is becoming less passive
rather than just gaming a single number.

00:05:30.157 --> 00:05:35.670
Variance is also lower than the baseline in several
cases, so the gains are stable."

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:39.129
"The ablations reveal how to use the method well.

00:05:39.209 --> 00:05:44.546
Targeting a single metric in isolation gives the
largest improvement on that metric,

00:05:44.626 --> 00:05:50.026
while combining metrics achieves a balance, tracing
out a Pareto front of trade-offs.

00:05:50.106 --> 00:05:54.024
Combining front, side, and distance-to-reference
works nicely,

00:05:54.104 --> 00:05:57.441
but adding rear collisions causes a clear regression.

00:05:57.521 --> 00:06:02.019
The authors trace this to false positives in the
rear-collision metric,

00:06:02.099 --> 00:06:06.210
caused by log-replayed agents that do not react in
the simulator.

00:06:06.290 --> 00:06:08.273
Two hyperparameters also matter.

00:06:08.353 --> 00:06:14.528
The early-stopping budget K of the identification
policy: ten epochs works best for single-metric

00:06:14.608 --> 00:06:17.042
error sets and twenty for multi-metric.

00:06:17.122 --> 00:06:22.072
And the upsampling factor: performance improves up
to about a factor of fifty,

00:06:22.152 --> 00:06:25.167
after which side collisions start to rise again,

00:06:25.247 --> 00:06:29.680
echoing a similar saturation observed in the Just
Train Twice method."

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:36.955
"To put concrete numbers on it: front collisions
drop from fourteen to nine against the strongest baseline,

00:06:37.035 --> 00:06:39.497
roughly a thirty-six percent reduction.

00:06:39.577 --> 00:06:44.712
Distance-to-reference-trajectory failures fall from
thirty-five to twenty-eight,

00:06:44.792 --> 00:06:46.863
about a twenty percent reduction.

00:06:46.943 --> 00:06:50.122
Side collisions go from fifty-five to forty-seven.

00:06:50.202 --> 00:06:55.662
Overall the paper reports improvements of around
thirty-five percent on some metrics.

00:06:55.742 --> 00:06:58.465
The best upsampling factor is around fifty.

00:06:58.545 --> 00:07:03.159
The models were trained on one hundred and eighty
hours of driving data,

00:07:03.239 --> 00:07:05.831
validated and tested on sixty hours each,

00:07:05.911 --> 00:07:08.960
with the final policy trained for forty epochs."

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:11.787
"The takeaway is refreshingly practical.

00:07:11.867 --> 00:07:15.829
You do not need a differentiable simulator, a human
in the loop,

00:07:15.909 --> 00:07:19.746
or expensive closed-loop training to get
closed-loop benefits.

00:07:19.826 --> 00:07:24.420
You just need to run your policy once in a
simulator, note where it fails,

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:26.631
upsample those scenes, and retrain.

00:07:26.711 --> 00:07:33.074
That simple recipe delivers significant reductions
in collisions and other non-differentiable metrics,

00:07:33.154 --> 00:07:37.180
works with any closed-loop metric, and adds no
inference latency.

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:43.054
And it comes with a clean theoretical story:
weighting scenes by failure is closely connected

00:07:43.134 --> 00:07:47.034
to correcting covariate shift through density-ratio
estimation,

00:07:47.114 --> 00:07:52.340
a promising direction for making imitation-learned
planners both simpler and safer."
